by nicolas-grekas at 2026-10-13T14:16:22Z

@Spomky here are the recipes that symfony/symfony#66636 removed from the KeyManagement README. The first one fits in "Data Key Stores", after the paragraph on the AAD. The second one fits in "Envelope Mode".

The example in "Additional Authenticated Data" has the gap that the first recipe fixes. With `$aad = 'tenant=acme'`, a payload of another column of the same tenant still opens.

````markdown
Refusing a payload written somewhere else
-----------------------------------------

An envelope says which data key opens it, and whoever can write the column chooses these bytes. A valid payload copied into another column, another tenant or another key scope still finds a valid data key. So the copy opens, unless the reader checks where the payload belongs. You can do this check with the existing API, on both formats.

For a payload written by `StoredEnvelopeEncrypter`, use the AAD. The whole scope uses the same data key, so the data key cannot tell two columns or two tenants apart. The AAD is bound to each payload, so put in it everything the payload must belong to, for example the column and the tenant:

```php
$aad = 'user.email:tenant:'.$tenantId;

$envelope = $encrypter->encrypt('user.email', $email, $aad);
$email = $encrypter->decrypt($envelope, $aad);
```

A payload written for another column or another tenant was sealed with another AAD, so it fails its tag check instead of opening. The reader must compute the AAD by itself, from the row it reads or from the request it serves. Never build the AAD from the envelope, for example from its key id. The envelope would then be checked against itself.

A payload that contains its own wrapped data key also contains the id of its master key. You can check this id before the KMS is called:

```php
if (null !== $envelope->keyId && !\in_array($envelope->keyId, $expectedKeyIds, true)) {
    throw new \RuntimeException('This payload was written under another master key.');
}
```

This check only refuses payloads written under another master key. For payloads written under the same master key, use the AAD as above. It works on this format too, when the backend can enforce an AAD.

The envelope contains the key id that the backend reports. This is not always the id given to `encrypt()`, and it can change over time:

 * AWS KMS records the ARN of the key behind an alias. This ARN changes when the alias is moved to another key, so add the new ARN to `$expectedKeyIds` before you move the alias.
 * Azure Key Vault records `name/version` when only a name is given. The version changes each time the key is rotated, so compare `strstr($envelope->keyId, '/', true)` with the name instead.

````

